O long-lived (Gambûsvâmin)! I (Sudharman) have heard the following Discourse from the Venerable (Mahâvîra):
Here, forsooth, the Venerable Ascetic Mahâvîra of the Kâsyapa Gôtra has declared twenty-two troubles which a monk must learn and know, bear and conquer, in order not to be vanquished by them when he lives the life of a wandering mendicant. These, then, are the twenty-two troubles declared by the Venerable Ascetic Mahâvîra, which a monk must learn and know, bear and conquer, in order not to be vanquished by them when he lives the life of a wandering mendicant:
digañkhâ (gugupsâ)-parîsahâ, hunger;
pivâsâ (pipâsâ)-p., thirst;
sîya (sîta)-p., cold;
usina (ushna)-p., heat;
damsamasaya (damsamasaka)-p., gad-flies, and gnats;
akêla-p., nakedness;
arati-p., to be discontented with the objects of control.
itthî (strî)-p., women;
kariyâ (karyâ)-p., erratic life;
nisîhiyâ (naishêdhikî)-p., place for study;
se.ggâ (sayyâ)-p., lodging;
akkôsa (âkrôsa)-p., abuse;
vaha (vadha)-p., corporal punishment;
gâyanâ (yâkanâ)-p., to ask for something;
alâbha-p., to be refused;
rôga-p., illness;
tana-phâsa (trinasparsa), pricking of grass;
galla-p., dirt;
sakkârapurakkâra (satkârapurahkâra)-p., kind and respectful treatment;
pannâ (pragñâ)-p., understanding;
annâna (agñâna)-p., ignorance;
sammatta (samyaktva)-p., righteousness.
The enumeration of the troubles has been delivered by the Kâsyapa, I shall explain them to you in due order. Listen to me.

O long-lived (Gambûsvâmin)! I (Sudharman) have heard the following discourse from the venerable (Mahâvîra).
Here, forsooth, the Venerable Ascetic Mahâvîra, of the Kasyapa Gôtra, has delivered this lecture called the exertion in righteousness. Many creatures, who truly believe in the subject (taught in this lecture), put their faith in it, give credence to it, accept it, practise it, comply with it, study it, understand it, learn it, and act up to it according to the precept (of the Ginas) --have obtained perfection, enlightenment, deliverance, final beatitude, and have put an end to all misery.
This lecture treats of the following subjects:
samvêga, longing for liberation;
nirvêda, disregard of worldly objects;
dharmasraddhâ, desire of the Law;
gurusâdharmikasusrûshanâ, obedience to co-religionists and to the Guru.
âlôkanâ, confession of sins before the Guru;
nindâ, repenting of one's sins to oneself;
garhâ, repenting of one's sins before the Guru;
sâmâyika, moral and intellectual purity of the soul;
katurvimsatistava, adoration of the twenty-four Ginas;
vandana, paying reverence to the Guru;
pratikramana, expiation of sins;
kâyôtsarga, a particular position of the body;
pratyâkhyâna, self-denial;
stavastutimaṅgala, praises and hymns;
kâlasya pratyupêkshanâ, keeping the right time;
prâyaskittakarana, practising penance;
kshamâpana, begging forgiveness;
svâdhyâya, study;
vâkanâ, recital of the sacred texts;
pariprikkhanâ, questioning (the teacher);
parâvartanâ, repetition;

anuprêkshâ, pondering;
dharmakathâ, religious discourse;
srutasyârâdhanâ, acquisition of sacred knowledge;
êkâgramanahsannivêsanâ, concentration of thoughts;
samyama, control;
tapas, austerities;
vyavadâna, cutting off the Karman;
sukhâsâta, renouncing pleasure;
apratibaddhatâ, mental independence;
vikitrasayanâsanasêvanâ, using unfrequented lodgings and beds;
vinivartanâ, turning from the world;
sambhôgapratyâkhyâna, renouncing collection of alms in one district only;
upadhipratyâkhyâna, renouncing articles of use;
âhârapratyâkhyâna, renouncing food;
kashâyapratyâkhyâna, conquering the passions;
yôgapratyâkhyâna, renouncing activity;
sarîrapratyâkhyâna, renouncing the body;
sahâyapratyâkhyâna, renouncing company;
bhaktapratyâkhyâna, renouncing all food;
sadbhâvapratyâkhyâna, perfect renunciation;
pratirûpatâ, conforming to the standard;
vaiyâvritya, doing service;
sarvagunasampûrnatâ, fulfilling all virtues;
vîtarâgatâ, freedom from passion;
kshânti, patience;
mukti, freedom from greed;
48, ârgava, simplicity;
mârdava, humility;
bhâvasatya, sincerity of mind;
karanasatya, sincerity of religious practice;
yôgasatya, sincerity of acting;
manôguptatâ, watchfulness of the mind;
vâg-guptatâ, watchfulness of the speech;
kâyaguptatâ, watchfulness of the body;
manahsamâdhâranâ, discipline of the mind;
vâksamâdhâranâ, discipline of the speech;
kâyasamâdhâranâ, discipline of the body;
gñânasampannatâ, possession of knowledge;
darsanasampannatâ, possession of faith;

kâritrasampannatâ, possession of conduct;
srôtrêndriyanigraha, subduing the ear;
kakshurindriyanigraha, subduing the eye;
ghrânêndriyanigraha, subduing the organ of smell;
gihvêndriyanigraha, subduing the tongue;
sparsanêndriyanigraha, subduing the organ of touch;
krôdhavigaya, conquering anger;
mânavigaya, conquering pride;
mâyâvigaya, conquering deceit;
lôbhavigaya, conquering greed;
prêmadvêshamithyâdarsanavigaya, conquering love, hate, and wrong belief;
sailêsî, stability;
akarmatâ, freedom from Karman.
Sir, what does the soul obtain by the longing for liberation? By the longing for liberation the soul obtains an intense desire of the Law; by an intense desire of the Law he quickly arrives at an (increased) longing for liberation; he destroys anger, pride, deceit, and greed, which reproduce themselves infinitely; he acquires no (bad) Karman, and ridding himself of wrong belief which is the consequence of the latter, he becomes possessed of right faith; by the purity of faith some will reach perfection after one birth; nobody, however, who has got this purity, will be born more than thrice before he reaches perfection.

Internal austerities are:
prâyaskitta, expiation of sins;
vinaya, politeness;
vaiyâvritya, serving the Guru;
svâdhyâya, study;
dhyâna, meditation;
vyutsarga, abandoning of the body.

The eight kinds of Karman are briefly the following:
Gñânâvaranîya (which acts as an obstruction to right knowledge);
Darsanâvaranîya (which acts as an obstruction to right faith);
Vêdanîya (which leads to experiencing pain or pleasure);
Môhanîya (which leads to delusion);
Âyuhkarman (which determines the length of life);
Nâman (which determines the name or individuality of the embodied soul);
Gôtra (which determines his Gôtra);
Antarâya (which prevents one's entrance on the path that leads to eternal bliss).

